CH = dict(num=10, title="The Constitution of India: An Introduction", tag="Political Science", theme="Making, sources, features and Preamble of the Constitution",
 intro="The Constitution is the rulebook of the nation. It sets up the government, protects citizens' rights, lists duties and states the values the country stands for. This chapter explains what a constitution is, how ours was made, what shaped it, its key features and the Preamble.",
 big_questions=["What is a constitution and why do we need one?", "How was the Indian Constitution prepared?", "How did the freedom struggle and civilisational heritage influence it?", "What are its key features, and why is it still relevant after seventy years?"],
 blocks=[
 ("h2", "10.1 What Is a Constitution?"),
 ("ul", ["The Constitution came into effect on **26 January 1950** (celebrated as **Republic Day**). The original is preserved in a **helium-filled glass case** in Parliament; helium does not react with paper or ink.",
  "A constitution is a document that spells out a nation's **basic principles and laws**. It lays out: (1) the framework and roles of the three organs (legislature, executive, judiciary); (2) **checks and balances** among them for fairness, responsibility and accountability; (3) citizens' **rights and duties**; (4) the nation's long-term goals and aspirations.",
  "The President takes an oath to *preserve, protect and defend* it; the PM, ministers and judges promise to follow it.",
  "**Why needed?** Like the kabaddi referee's rulebook, it lets everyone settle disputes by agreed rules. It decides what kind of government there will be, how it is formed and how it works, and states values such as equality, justice, fraternity, pluralism and freedom, which form the basis of laws.",
  "Size: **25 Parts and 12 Schedules** now (22 Parts and 8 Schedules in 1950); the **world's largest written constitution**."]),
 ("table", ["Constitution deals with", ""], [
  ["Values and ideals of a society", "Rights and duties of citizens"],
  ["Structure and formation of government", "Functions of different parts of government"],
  ["Political system", "Guiding principles for government"]]),
 ("h2", "10.2 Writing the Constitution"),
 ("ul", ["While the freedom struggle continued, planning was needed: type of government, guiding rules, who votes, how disputes are settled.",
  "**Constituent Assembly**: formed **9 December 1946** with **389** members (reduced to **299** after Partition), **15 women**; members represented regions, professions and social groups and were elected by the provincial legislative assemblies (themselves elected by the people).",
  "Temporary president at the start: **Dr. Sachidananda Sinha**. Chairman: **Dr. Rajendra Prasad** (later first President of India).",
  "**Drafting Committee** chaired by **Dr. B. R. Ambedkar** (social reformer, first Law and Justice Minister).",
  "Took almost **three years**; adopted on **26 November 1949**; came into force on **26 January 1950**.",
  "The text was hand-written by calligrapher **Prem Behari Narain Raizada**, and **Nandalal Bose** and his team illustrated it with scenes from Indian history (gurukula, Ramayana, Mahabharata, Nataraja, Mahabalipuram, Akbar, Nalanda, Mohenjo-daro to the freedom movement)."]),
 ("h2", "10.3 What Shaped the Constitution?"),
 ("table", ["Influence", "What it gave"], [
  ["Freedom movement", "Ideals of equality, justice, freedom, fraternity, preserving cultural heritage; many freedom fighters sat in the Assembly. Answers to questions on universal adult franchise, separation of legislature and judiciary, fundamental rights, amendment process, powers of Centre and States"],
  ["Civilisational heritage", "Acceptance of diverse viewpoints, Nature as sacred, love of learning, respect for women, *vasudhaiva kutumbakam* ('world is one family'), *sarve bhavantu sukhinah*. Past governance experiments (janapadas, sanghas, saptanga, rajadharma) stressed duties, so **Fundamental Duties** were included"],
  ["Other countries' constitutions", "Studied France, USA, UK, Ireland, Australia ('Let noble thoughts come to us from every side'). **Liberty, equality, fraternity** from **France** (French Revolution, 1789); **Directive Principles of State Policy** from **Ireland**; the American Constitution helped shape an **independent judiciary**"]]),
 ("h2", "10.4 Key Features"),
 ("ul", ["**Three organs** with separation of powers: legislature makes laws; executive (headed by the PM) implements; judiciary checks laws against the Constitution and decides on breaches and punishment.",
  "**Three-tier government**: central, state and local (Panchayati Raj).",
  "**Division of powers** between Centre and States; defined roles and accountability for each organ.",
  "**Electoral system** ensuring every eligible citizen can vote (universal adult franchise from the beginning).",
  "**Fundamental Rights, Fundamental Duties and Directive Principles.**"]),
 ("table", ["", "Fundamental Rights", "Directive Principles (DPSP)", "Fundamental Duties"], [
  ["Nature", "Promises that **must be kept**; can be enforced in court", "Goals / guidelines for the government; **not enforceable** in court", "Responsibilities of citizens (Part IV-A, added **1976**)"],
  ["Examples", "Equality (Art. 14: equality before law); Freedom (Art. 21: life and personal liberty); against Exploitation; Art. 21-A: right to education", "Art. 38 social, political, economic justice; Art. 41 welfare; Art. 44 Uniform Civil Code; Art. 47 nutrition and health; Art. 48-A environment and wildlife; Art. 49 protecting monuments", "Abide by the Constitution, flag and anthem; defend the country; preserve composite culture; protect environment and have compassion for living creatures; strive for excellence; parents to provide education to children aged 6-14"]]),
 ("note", "REMEMBER", "Fundamental Rights = demandable now; DPSP = bigger goals that take time. Begum Aizaz Rasul (22 Nov 1949) said equality of women is no new concept in India but an ancient ideal."),
 ("h2", "10.5 A Living Document"),
 ("ul", ["The makers knew new needs would arise, so changes (**amendments**) are allowed after rigorous debate in Parliament; some also need state legislatures; public opinion is sought; popular movements can start change.",
  "Examples: **Fundamental Duties** added in 1976; **Panchayati Raj** added in 1992 by the **73rd Amendment**; in **2004** the Supreme Court held that flying the national flag is part of freedom of expression after a citizen's challenge."]),
 ("h2", "10.6 The Preamble"),
 ("p", "The Preamble summarises the guiding values. It begins 'We, the people of India', meaning the Constitution was drawn up by the people through their representatives, not handed down by a king or outside power."),
 ("table", ["Word", "Meaning"], [
  ["Sovereign", "People have supreme right to decide internal and external matters; no external power can dictate"],
  ["Socialist", "Wealth is created socially and shared; government regulates ownership of land and industry to reduce inequality"],
  ["Secular", "Complete freedom to follow any religion; no official religion; state treats all faiths with equal respect"],
  ["Democratic", "People enjoy equal political rights, elect rulers and hold them accountable; governed by basic rules"],
  ["Republic", "Head of the state is elected, not hereditary"],
  ["Justice", "No discrimination by caste, religion or gender; reduce social inequalities; welfare of all, especially the less advantaged"],
  ["Liberty", "No unreasonable restrictions on thought, expression and action"],
  ["Equality", "All equal before the law; end social inequalities; equal opportunity"],
  ["Fraternity", "Behave as members of one family; no citizen is inferior"]]),
 ("note", "IMPORTANT", "The words **'Socialist'** and **'Secular'** were added to the Preamble by the **42nd Amendment, 1976**."),
 ("h2", "10.7 Quick Revision"),
 ("qa", [("What is a constitution?", "A document stating a nation's basic principles and laws: organs of government, checks and balances, rights and duties, and long-term goals."),
  ("Why do we need a constitution?", "It is a rulebook that settles disputes, defines the government and protects rights, so everyone agrees on common rules."),
  ("When did the Constitution come into force?", "26 January 1950; celebrated as Republic Day."),
  ("Why is it preserved in a helium-filled case?", "Helium does not react with paper or ink, so the document is preserved."),
  ("Write about the Constituent Assembly.", "Formed 9 Dec 1946 with 389 members (299 after Partition), 15 women; Rajendra Prasad chaired; Ambedkar headed the Drafting Committee; adopted 26 Nov 1949."),
  ("What influenced the Constitution?", "The freedom struggle, India's civilisational heritage and constitutions of other democracies."),
  ("What was borrowed from other countries?", "Liberty-equality-fraternity from France; Directive Principles from Ireland; independent judiciary ideas from the USA."),
  ("Differentiate Fundamental Rights and Directive Principles.", "Rights are enforceable in court; DPSP are guidelines that the government should strive to achieve."),
  ("What are Fundamental Duties? When added?", "Citizens' responsibilities (e.g. respecting the flag, protecting environment); added in 1976."),
  ("Why is the Constitution called a living document?", "It can be amended as needs change, e.g. Panchayati Raj added by the 73rd Amendment in 1992."),
  ("Explain the terms Sovereign, Secular and Republic.", "Sovereign: independent decision-making; Secular: no state religion, equal respect; Republic: elected head of state."),
  ("Which words were added to the Preamble in 1976?", "Socialist and Secular, through the 42nd Amendment."),
  ("Who wrote and who illustrated the Constitution?", "Prem Behari Narain Raizada wrote it; Nandalal Bose and team illustrated it.")]),
 ],
 takeaways=["The Constitution is the **rulebook** of the nation; in force since **26 Jan 1950**.",
  "Made by the **Constituent Assembly** (Dec 1946 - Nov 1949); **Ambedkar** chaired the Drafting Committee.",
  "Shaped by the **freedom struggle**, India's **heritage** and **other constitutions**.",
  "Key features: three organs, three tiers, **Fundamental Rights, DPSP, Fundamental Duties**.",
  "It is a **living document** that can be amended.",
  "**Preamble**: sovereign, socialist, secular, democratic, republic; justice, liberty, equality, fraternity."])
